-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('305021c6-38c8-51ce-b9ed-cde79133922e', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Offline$q$, $q$The Pine Hollow library had one computer lab, six laptops, and an internet connection that slowed to a crawl whenever more than two people used it. It was also the only place in town with a table long enough for the robotics club to build on.

Kiri Walker, the club's captain, had planned to download a ready-made program for their robot's arm. Teams in the city did it all the time. But on Tuesday, the download froze at eleven percent for the third time, and the librarian, Mr. Osei, gently reminded her that the lab closed at six.

"We have four days until the regional contest," Kiri said. She pressed her forehead against the cool table. "We can't even get the program."

Her teammate Dev Raman was quiet for a moment. "Then we write it ourselves," he said.

Kiri laughed, but he did not. Dev pulled out a spiral notebook full of diagrams. He had been sketching how the arm's motors should move, joint by joint, during the long afternoons when the internet was too slow to do anything else.

For the next three days, the team worked on paper first. They tested each idea by hand, moving the robot's arm slowly and writing down every angle. Only when a section was finished did they type it into a laptop, which needed no internet to run their code.

At the contest, a judge asked where they had found their program.

"We wrote it," Kiri said. "Every line."

They placed fourth out of twenty teams. On the drive home, Kiri asked Dev to be co-captain. She had learned that the person who talked the least was sometimes the one who had planned the most.$q$, 'draft')
