-- Passage rg7-character-setting-plot-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0976af9e-d4f2-5d97-83bb-89e50a67d44b', 'rg7-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wall on Ferris Avenue$q$, $q$Priya Nair had designed the mural for the wall of the old bus depot on Ferris Avenue, and the city arts council had approved every color. The plan was simple: she and five volunteers would paint for two weekends in early May.

But that May, it rained almost every day. Paint would not dry on the damp bricks, and the first weekend was washed out completely. On the second Saturday, Priya stood under the depot's narrow overhang with her sketches and watched water run down the gray wall.

The only dry spot outside was under the overhang itself, a strip about three feet deep. Bus riders crowded there every afternoon to stay out of the rain.

"We could paint the overhang's ceiling," said Mr. Castellano, a retired bus driver who had volunteered. "It stays dry. And everyone waiting would look up at it."

Priya frowned. The ceiling was small and low. Her design had a long river running the whole length of the wall, with the neighborhood's houses along its banks. None of that would fit.

That night she redrew the plan. Instead of one river, she painted a sky full of paper kites, each one carrying a tiny picture of a street in the neighborhood. Bus riders waiting under the overhang began suggesting streets for her to add. A boy named Tobias asked for his grandmother's bakery. A woman named Rosa asked for the corner where she had learned to ride a bike.

By June, the rain had stopped, and the council asked if Priya still wanted to paint the big wall. She did. But she kept one thing from the ceiling: along the river, each house now had a kite flying above it, chosen by a rider who had once waited out the rain under the overhang.$q$, 'draft')
